        "id": "notes",
        "position": [
          2,
          1
        ],
        "params": {
          "text": "**SP11 \u2014 When did that frequency happen?**  \n**Predict \u2192 Run \u2192 Inspect \u2192 Change duration. Run 4 s at 128 Hz.** The 32 Hz burst occupies n=128\u2026255; a quieter chirp rises from 8 toward 40 Hz.  \nOpen a map. Select STFT, FFT size 64, Hop 32 samples, Magnitude. Expect 15 columns, \u0394f=2 Hz and 0.25 s between centers; the first center is 0.25390625 s.  \nCompare N=128 / Hop 64, then N=64 / Hop 16. Smaller hop adds columns, not sharper frequency resolution. Only full windows are analyzed; edge overlap spreads the apparent burst.  \nSelect stop, F4: start_time 2 \u2192 1.5 s. Predict the shorter burst before rerunning. Scope observes source sample n at model time (n+1)/128 s.  \n[Open SP11 student lab](qrc:/s7studio/help/education/labs/SP11-short-time-fourier/README.md) \u00b7 [MIT STFT lecture](https://sigproc.mit.edu/_static/fall25/lectures/Short-Time_Fourier_Transform.pdf) \u00b7 [S7 time\u2013frequency guide](qrc:/s7studio/help/examples/time_frequency/README.md)",
          "text_format": "markdown",
          "width": 1670,
          "height": 500,
          "font_size": 20
        }
      }
    ]
  }
